= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** Take $k(x)=1/(1+|x|)$. This is bounded and measurable on $\mathbb R$, and tends to zero as $x\to\infty$, but
$$
\int_0^Rk(x)\,dx=\log(1+R)\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
Thus decay to zero alone does not imply <Lebesgue integrability>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** Let $k$ be the indicator of the positive integers. It is measurable, bounded by one and zero <almost everywhere>, so its <Lebesgue integral> on $(0,\infty)$ is zero. Yet $k(n)=1$ for every positive integer $n$, so it does not tend to zero at infinity. <Lebesgue integrability> is unchanged by modifications on a <null set>, whereas a pointwise limit can change.

= b
{parent=D3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $H_0=\int_0^\pi|h(u)|\,du<\infty$. On each period, substitute $x=j\pi+u$ and use periodicity:
$$
\int_{j\pi}^{(j+1)\pi}e^{-\beta x}|h(x)|\,dx
=e^{-\beta j\pi}\int_0^\pi e^{-\beta u}|h(u)|\,du
\leq e^{-\beta j\pi}H_0.
$$
Summing over $j\geq0$ gives the convergent geometric bound
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty e^{-\beta x}|h(x)|\,dx\leq\frac{H_0}{1-e^{-\beta\pi}}<\infty.}
$$
Measurability is preserved under multiplication by the exponential, so this proves <Lebesgue integrability>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the nonnegative parts $h^+=\max(h,0)$ and $h^-=\max(-h,0)$, so $h=h^+-h^-$. The zero-mean assumption gives $H:=\int_0^\pi h^+=\int_0^\pi h^-<\infty$. On the $k$-th period, each of the positive and negative weighted integrals lies between $H/((k+1)\pi)$ and $H/(k\pi)$. Their difference therefore satisfies the stronger estimate
$$
\left|\int_{k\pi}^{(k+1)\pi}\frac{h(x)}x\,dx\right|
\leq\frac H\pi\left(\frac1k-\frac1{k+1}\right)
=\frac H{\pi k(k+1)}\leq\boxed{\frac H{\pi k^2}}.
$$
The sum of the absolute values of these block integrals converges, proving the existence of the finite limit of the integrals from $\pi$ to $n\pi$.

For genuine <Lebesgue integrability> we instead need the integral of the absolute value. Periodicity gives
$$
\int_{k\pi}^{(k+1)\pi}\frac{|h(x)|}x\,dx\geq\frac{2H}{(k+1)\pi}.
$$
If $H>0$, summation diverges by the <harmonic series>. If $H=0$, then $h=0$ <almost everywhere> and the function is integrable. Thus
$$
\boxed{g\in L^1(\pi,\infty)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad h=0\text{ almost everywhere}.}
$$
The finite signed block limit therefore need not be a <Lebesgue integral>.

