= Solution
{parent=i}

The $j$-th <partial derivative> is the <vector> limit
$$
\partial_jf(x)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+he_j)-f(x)}h,
$$
when it exists. The <total derivative> is a <linear map> $Df(x):\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$ such that
$$
f(x+h)=f(x)+Df(x)h+o(\|h\|).
$$
The <Jacobian matrix> is $J_f(x)=(\partial_jf_i(x))_{i,j}$. When the <total derivative> exists, all partial derivatives exist and $J_f(x)$ is its <matrix> in the standard bases; its $j$-th column is $Df(x)e_j$. Existence of partial derivatives alone does not guarantee total differentiability.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a scalar-valued function, the <gradient> is the column <vector>
$$
\boxed{\nabla f(x)=(\partial_1f(x),\ldots,\partial_nf(x))^T.}
$$
When $f$ is differentiable, its <total derivative> is the <linear functional>
$$
\boxed{Df(x)h=\nabla f(x)\cdot h.}
$$
Thus the Jacobian is the transpose of the <gradient> in standard Euclidean coordinates.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <chain rule> at the specified point is
$$
\boxed{D(f\circ g)(x)=Df(g(x))\circ Dg(x).}
$$
In standard coordinates this becomes
$$
\boxed{J_{f\circ g}(x)=J_f(g(x))J_g(x).}
$$
The <matrix> dimensions are respectively $k\times n$, $k\times m$, and $m\times n$, <matching> the composition of the two linear derivative maps.

= b
{parent=F2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $Q=1+x^2+y^2>0$. Differentiation of $Q^{-1}$ gives
$$
\boxed{\nabla f(x,y)=\frac{-2}{(1+x^2+y^2)^2}\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The positive denominator also shows that the function and these derivatives are smooth throughout $\mathbb R^2$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $(-1,0)$ the <gradient> is $(1/2,0)^T$. The <total derivative> is therefore the <linear functional>
$$
\boxed{Df(-1,0)(h,k)=\frac h2,\qquad J_f(-1,0)=\begin{pmatrix}1/2&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The derivative of the curve is
$$
\boxed{Dg(t)h=h\begin{pmatrix}\cos t\\-\sin t\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Its image lies on the unit circle, so $f(g(t))=1/2$ and $\boxed{D(f\circ g)(t)=0}$. Meanwhile
$$
J_f(g(t))=\begin{pmatrix}-\sin t/2&-\cos t/2\end{pmatrix},
$$
and multiplication by $J_g(t)$ gives $-\sin t\cos t/2+\cos t\sin t/2=0$. This verifies the <chain rule> explicitly.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Because $2|x|\leq1+x^2\leq Q$, we have $|f_x(x,y)|=2|x|/Q^2\leq1/Q\leq1$; similarly $|f_y|\leq1$. Apply the one-variable <mean value theorem> on the horizontal and vertical segments joining $(x,y)$ to $(u,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The <triangle inequality> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
|f(x,y)-f(u,v)|&\leq|f(x,y)-f(u,y)|+|f(u,y)-f(u,v)|\\
&\leq\boxed{|x-u|+|y-v|}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the function is globally one-Lipschitz for the $\ell^1$ metric on the plane.

