= Solution
{parent=i}

The <moment-generating function> is $M_X(t)=\mathbb E[e^{tX}]$ for real $t$ where this <expectation> is finite. Usually one requires finiteness on a neighbourhood of zero when using it to characterize a distribution. The <characteristic function> is
$$
\boxed{\varphi_X(t)=\mathbb E[e^{itX}],\quad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
It always exists because the integrand has absolute value one. In both definitions the <expectation> is taken with respect to the distribution of $X$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <characteristic function> is useful for proving convergence in distribution, including for laws without a finite <moment-generating function>: the continuity theorem says that pointwise convergence of characteristic functions to a <characteristic function> continuous at zero implies convergence in distribution to its law. For example, the <Cauchy distribution> has a <characteristic function> although its <moment-generating function> is not finite on any neighbourhood of zero.

A <moment-generating function> is especially useful for exponential tail bounds: for $t>0$ where it is finite, <Markov's inequality> gives
$$
\boxed{\Pr(X\geq a)\leq e^{-ta}M_X(t).}
$$
Optimizing over $t$ yields a <Chernoff bound>. The positive real exponential in this argument permits the inequality; a unit-modulus complex exponential does not give the corresponding tail bound.

= b
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Expand the analytic factor around zero by the <binomial series>:
$$
(14-5z)^{-1/2}=\frac1{\sqrt{14}}\sum_{j=0}^\infty
\frac{\binom{2j}{j}}{4^j}\left(\frac5{14}\right)^jz^j.
$$
Every coefficient is nonnegative. Adding $2/3$ to the constant coefficient gives probabilities $p_j$ with sum $g(1)=2/3+1/3=1$. Define $\Pr(T=j)=p_j$ for $j\geq0$. This is a nonnegative integer-valued <random variable> and its <probability generating function> is exactly $g$. The power series has radius $14/5>1$, so the evaluations and derivatives used below are finite.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a <probability generating function>, $\mathbb ET=g'(1)$ and $\mathbb E[T(T-1)]=g''(1)$. Here
$$
g'(z)=\frac52(14-5z)^{-3/2},\qquad
g''(z)=\frac{75}4(14-5z)^{-5/2}.
$$
Thus $\mathbb ET=5/54$ and $\mathbb E[T(T-1)]=25/324$. Adding the first moment gives $\mathbb ET^2=55/324$, hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb ET=\frac5{54},\qquad\operatorname{Var}(T)=\frac{55}{324}-\frac{25}{2916}=\frac{235}{1458}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

<Markov's inequality> states that for a nonnegative <random variable> $Z$ and $a>0$,
$$
\boxed{\Pr(Z\geq a)\leq\frac{\mathbb EZ}{a}.}
$$
It follows immediately from $Z\geq a\mathbf1_{\{Z\geq a\}}$ and monotonicity of <expectation>. A finite <expectation> makes the bound informative.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

By the definition of the <probability generating function>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[2^T]=g(2)=\frac23+(14-10)^{-1/2}=\frac76.}
$$
The evaluation at two is valid because two is inside the power-series radius $14/5$.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The event $T\geq5$ is the same as $2^T\geq32$. Apply <Markov's inequality> to the nonnegative <random variable> $2^T$ and use part (iv):
$$
\boxed{\Pr(T\geq5)\leq\frac{7/6}{32}=\frac7{192}\approx0.03646<0.05.}
$$
The strict numerical comparison is enough even though the probabilistic inequality itself is non-strict.

= c
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At each index the two independent uniform symbols match with <probability> $p=k/k^2=1/k$. The match indicators are independent across indices. Consequently the waiting times between successive matches are independent geometric random variables on $1,2,\ldots$ with success <probability> $p$, and $S_m$ is their sum.

For one waiting time $G$, summing the <geometric series> gives $\mathbb Ee^{tG}=pe^t/[1-(1-p)e^t]$. Independence yields
$$
\boxed{M_{S_m}(t)=\left(\frac{e^t/k}{1-(1-1/k)e^t}\right)^m,\qquad
\mathbb ES_m=mk.}
$$
For $k>1$ the <moment-generating function> is finite for $t<\log(k/(k-1))$. When $k=1$, every index matches, $S_m=m$ deterministically and the formula reduces to $e^{mt}$ for every real $t$.

= d
{parent=M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $t\geq0$, the geometric survival <probability> gives
$$
\Pr(T_k>t)=\Pr(S_1>kt)=\left(1-\frac1k\right)^{\lfloor kt\rfloor}\longrightarrow e^{-t}.
$$
For $t<0$, the distribution functions are zero, as is the limiting <exponential distribution> function. Since that limiting distribution function is continuous everywhere, these limits prove
$$
\boxed{T_k\xrightarrow{d}\operatorname{Exp}(1).}
$$
The parameter is rate one, so the limiting mean is one, consistent with $\mathbb E(S_1/k)=1$.

= M5
{parent=M}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Given $X_n=k$, the next child takes a fresh uniform choice from $1,\ldots,k$, making $X_{n+1}$ uniform on $0,\ldots,k-1$. At zero the count stays zero. This conditional law depends only on the current count, so $X_n$ is a <Markov chain>.

The amounts taken do not generally retain that state information. For $N\geq3$, compare two positive-<probability> histories with the same current value $Y_2=1$: if $Y_1=N-2$, exactly one sticker remains and $Y_3=1$; if $Y_1=N-1$, none remains and $Y_3=0$. Thus the next amount depends on earlier history even after conditioning on the current amount, violating the <Markov property>.

The non-Markov assertion needs this nondegenerate range of $N$. With $N=1$ the amount process is trivial; for $N=2$ it is also Markov if time-inhomogeneous transitions are permitted. The history counterexample establishes the intended failure for every $N\geq3$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The state space is $\{0,1,\ldots,N\}$. In this increasing ordering, the <transition matrix> is specified by
$$
\boxed{P_{00}=1,\qquad P_{kj}=\frac1k\ (k\geq1,\ 0\leq j<k),\qquad P_{kj}=0\text{ otherwise}.}
$$
For each positive $k$, the $k$ possible amounts taken correspond bijectively to the $k$ possible remaining counts. Each positive-state row therefore sums to one.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A state is a <recurrent state> if the <probability> of a return at some positive time, starting there, is one. It is a <transient state> if that return <probability> is less than one. It is an <absorbing state> if its one-step <probability> of remaining there is one. An <absorbing state> is recurrent, since every subsequent time is a return.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

State zero is absorbing and recurrent. Every positive state is transient: after leaving it, the sticker count strictly decreases until reaching zero and can never return to that positive value. In fact absorption occurs after at most $N$ children who take a positive amount. Hence
$$
\boxed{0\text{ is the sole recurrent and absorbing state; }1,\ldots,N\text{ are transient}.}
$$

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Let $e_k$ be the expected number of future children receiving stickers from a box containing $k$, with $e_0=0$. Conditioning on the first uniform remainder gives
$$
e_k=1+\frac1k\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}e_j.
$$
Subtract the corresponding identity for $k-1$ after multiplying each by its denominator. This gives $ke_k=k e_{k-1}+1$, so $e_k=e_{k-1}+1/k$. Therefore $e_N=\sum_{j=1}^N1/j$, and
$$
\boxed{e_4=1+\frac12+\frac13+\frac14=\frac{25}{12}.}
$$
The recurrence counts the current positive recipient once and excludes all later children arriving after the box is empty.

= vi
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

The remainder after the first child, $X_2$, is uniform on $0,\ldots,N-1$. Given $X_2=j\geq1$, the second child takes an average of $(j+1)/2$ stickers; when $j=0$ the amount is zero. Hence, for $N\geq1$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EY_2=\frac1N\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}\frac{j+1}{2}
=\frac{(N-1)(N+2)}{4N}.}
$$
The expression gives zero for $N=1$, as expected.

= b
{parent=M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Since refilling is immediate, the observed arrival-state space becomes $\{1,\ldots,N\}$. A zero remainder is replaced by $N$, while all positive remainders are unchanged. Thus
$$
\boxed{P_{kN}=\frac1k,\qquad P_{kj}=\frac1k\ (1\leq j<k),\qquad P_{kj}=0\text{ otherwise}.}
$$
In particular state one moves to $N$ with <probability> one, and state $N$ has a self-loop of <probability> $1/N$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $Q=\sum_{m=1}^N1/m$ and propose $\pi_N=1/Q$, $\pi_j=1/((j+1)Q)$ for $j<N$. These probabilities sum to one because $1+\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}1/(j+1)=Q$.

For $j<N$, the incoming stationary <probability> is
$$
\sum_{k=j+1}^N\frac{\pi_k}{k}
=\frac1Q\left\{\sum_{k=j+1}^{N-1}\frac1{k(k+1)}+\frac1N\right\}
=\frac1{(j+1)Q}=\pi_j,
$$
using the telescoping identity $1/(k(k+1))=1/k-1/(k+1)$. Similarly $\sum_{k=1}^N\pi_k/k=1/Q=\pi_N$. Thus $\pi$ is stationary.

The finite chain is irreducible: every state can reach $N$, and from $N$ every state is reachable in one step. Its self-loop at $N$ makes it aperiodic. The finite-state convergence theorem therefore gives the claimed limit from any initial state:
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\Pr(X_n=j)=\begin{cases}1/((j+1)Q),&1\leq j<N,\\1/Q,&j=N.\end{cases}}
$$
For $N=1$ this is the single absorbing refill state and the same formula gives <probability> one.

= O
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= O4
{parent=O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Ignoring the multinomial coefficient, the likelihood is $L(\theta)=\prod_{i,j}\theta_{ij}^{n_{ij}}$. Maximize $\ell=\sum_{i,j}n_{ij}\log\theta_{ij}$ with $\sum\theta_{ij}=1$. A <Lagrange multiplier> gives $n_{ij}/\theta_{ij}=\lambda$; summing the resulting $\theta_{ij}=n_{ij}/\lambda$ gives $\lambda=N$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{ij}=\frac{n_{ij}}N.}
$$
Because every count is positive, the <log-likelihood> is strictly concave on the interior simplex and this solution is its unique maximum.

= b
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Under the product constraint the <log-likelihood> separates:
$$
\ell(\alpha,\beta)=\sum_i n_{i.}\log\alpha_i+\sum_jn_{.j}\log\beta_j.
$$
Maximizing the two simplex problems independently gives $\widehat\alpha_i=n_{i.}/N$ and $\widehat\beta_j=n_{.j}/N$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{ij}^{\,0}=\frac{n_{i.}n_{.j}}{N^2}.}
$$
The fitted expected cell counts under independence are consequently $N\widehat\theta_{ij}^{\,0}=n_{i.}n_{.j}/N$.

= c
{parent=O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The generalized <likelihood ratio> compares the constrained maximum from part (b) with the unrestricted maximum from part (a). Taking minus twice its logarithm gives
$$
\boxed{\Lambda=2\sum_{i,j}n_{ij}\log\frac{n_{ij}N}{n_{i.}n_{.j}}.}
$$
The unrestricted $3\times3$ model has $9-1=8$ free parameters; the independence model has $(3-1)+(3-1)=4$. Thus the asymptotic null distribution is $\chi^2_4$, with four degrees of freedom. The supplied value gives
$$
p\text{-value}\approx1-F_4(11.8)=0.02.
$$
Therefore **reject independence at the 5% level**, but not at the 1% level. There is evidence of an association between the two classifications. The fitted expected counts are all about nine or larger, so the usual chi-squared approximation has no very small expected cells here.

= O5
{parent=O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The normal-error <log-likelihood>, for fixed <variance>, is a constant minus $\|Y-X\theta\|^2/(2\sigma^2)$. Differentiating the squared residual gives the normal equations $X^TX\widehat\theta=X^TY$. Since $X$ has full column rank, $X^TX$ is positive definite and the squared residual is strictly convex in $\theta$. Hence the unique minimizer, and thus the <maximum-likelihood estimator>, is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY.}
$$
Profiling over the unknown positive <variance> does not change the residual-minimizing value of $\theta$.

= b
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $A=(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$. Since $AX=I$, the estimator is $\widehat\theta=\theta+A\epsilon$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E\widehat\theta=\theta,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta)=A(\sigma^2I)A^T=\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}.}
$$
Its <expectation> proves unbiasedness. It is also jointly normal, being an affine transformation of the normal error <vector>.

= c
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\mathrm{RSS}=\|Y-X\widehat\theta\|^2$. The usual unbiased error-<variance> estimator is
$$
\boxed{s^2=\frac{\mathrm{RSS}}{n-p}.}
$$
Indeed the residual is the <orthogonal projection> of $\epsilon$ onto the $(n-p)$-dimensional complement of the design column space, giving $\mathrm{RSS}/\sigma^2\sim\chi^2_{n-p}$ and expected RSS equal to $(n-p)\sigma^2$. The <maximum-likelihood estimator> instead divides RSS by $n$; the unbiased divisor above is the one used for the exact t-interval in part (d).

= d
{parent=O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $C=(X^TX)^{-1}$ and $\nu=n-p$. The normal law of the estimator gives $(\widehat\theta_i-\theta_i)/(\sigma\sqrt{C_{ii}})\sim N(0,1)$. The fitted and residual Gaussian projections are <orthogonal> and hence independent, so this quantity is independent of $\nu s^2/\sigma^2\sim\chi^2_\nu$. Their ratio therefore has <Student's t-distribution>:
$$
\frac{\widehat\theta_i-\theta_i}{s\sqrt{C_{ii}}}\sim t_\nu.
$$
If $t_{\nu,1-\alpha/2}$ is its upper $1-\alpha/2$ <quantile>, rearranging its central <probability> event gives
$$
\boxed{\left[\widehat\theta_i-t_{\nu,1-\alpha/2}s\sqrt{C_{ii}},\quad
\widehat\theta_i+t_{\nu,1-\alpha/2}s\sqrt{C_{ii}}\right].}
$$
This interval has exact coverage $1-\alpha$ under the given normal linear model, rather than merely large-sample coverage.

= P
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= P3
{parent=P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The best <polynomial> minimizes the weighted squared error among all polynomials of degree at most $n$:
$$
\boxed{\|u-p_n\|\leq\|u-q\|\quad\text{for every }q\in\Pi_n.}
$$
The given <inner product> is positive definite on continuous functions despite the weight vanishing at zero: a continuous nonzero function is nonzero on an interval containing nonzero points, so its weighted square integral is strictly positive.

= b
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If the error is <orthogonal> to $\Pi_n$, then for any $q\in\Pi_n$,
$$
\|u-q\|^2=\|u-p_n\|^2+\|p_n-q\|^2\geq\|u-p_n\|^2,
$$
proving sufficiency. Conversely, if $p_n$ minimizes, perturb it to $p_n+tq$ for any real $t$. The squared error becomes
$$
\|u-p_n\|^2-2t(u-p_n,q)+t^2\|q\|^2.
$$
A minimum at $t=0$ forces the linear coefficient to vanish, proving necessity.

For existence, choose the <basis> $1,x,\ldots,x^n$. Its <Gram matrix> $G$ is positive definite because $c^TGc$ is the squared <norm> of the nonzero <polynomial> with coefficients $c$. Thus $G$ is invertible, and the normal equations $Gc=((u,x^j))_{j=0}^n$ have a solution, giving an <orthogonal> residual and therefore a minimizer. The Pythagorean identity shows any other minimizer differs by a <polynomial> of zero <norm>, so it is equal to the first. Hence the best <polynomial> exists and is unique.

= c
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For odd $u$, define $q(x)=-p_n(-x)$. It is another <polynomial> in $\Pi_n$. Changing variables $x\mapsto-x$, and using evenness of the weight $x^2$, gives $\|u-q\|=\|u-p_n\|$. Thus $q$ is also a best <polynomial>. Uniqueness from part (b) gives $p_n(x)=-p_n(-x)$, proving
$$
\boxed{p_n\text{ is odd}.}
$$
Equivalently, the even component of a candidate is <orthogonal> to the odd residual and can only increase the squared error.

= d
{parent=P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

By part (c), the best degree-at-most-two <polynomial> is odd, so it must be $p_2(x)=bx$. <Orthogonality> to $x$ gives
$$
b=\frac{(x^{2011},x)}{(x,x)}
=\frac{\int_{-1}^1x^{2014}\,dx}{\int_{-1}^1x^4\,dx}
=\frac{2/2015}{2/5}=\frac1{403}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_2(x)=\frac{x}{403}.}
$$
Its error is automatically <orthogonal> to the even <basis> functions $1,x^2$ by parity, and the chosen coefficient makes it <orthogonal> to $x$, so part (b) verifies optimality.

= P4
{parent=P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For nonzero $w$, the <Householder transformation> is
$$
\boxed{H(w)=I-2\frac{ww^T}{w^Tw}.}
$$
The rank-one <matrix> $P=ww^T/(w^Tw)$ is symmetric and satisfies $P^2=P$. Consequently $H^T=H$ and $H^TH=(I-2P)^2=I-4P+4P^2=I$. Thus the <matrix> is symmetric and <orthogonal>; geometrically it reflects the component along $w$ and fixes its perpendicular hyperplane.

= b
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $r=\|u\|_2>0$ and choose $s=1$ when $u_1\geq0$, otherwise $s=-1$. Set $w=u+sr e_1$, which is nonzero because $r+su_1>0$. Compute
$$
w^Tu=r(r+su_1),\qquad w^Tw=2r(r+su_1).
$$
Thus the reflecting term in $H(w)u$ is exactly $w$, giving
$$
\boxed{H(w)u=u-w=-sr e_1.}
$$
Its first coordinate is nonzero and all remaining coordinates vanish. The choice of sign also avoids subtracting nearly equal vectors when the first component is large.

= c
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply part (b) to the first column of $A$, using an <orthogonal> reflection to zero all entries below the first. Next apply a <Householder reflection> to the trailing subvector of the second column, embedded as $\operatorname{diag}(1,H_2)$ so it leaves the first row and the zeros already created intact. Continue similarly through the columns. If a trailing column subvector is zero, skip that step.

The product of these embedded <orthogonal> matrices sends $A$ to an <upper triangular matrix> $R$. Write that product as $Q^T$; its transpose $Q$ is also <orthogonal>. Hence
$$
\boxed{Q^TA=R,\qquad A=QR.}
$$
The argument uses no inverse of $A$ and therefore includes singular matrices.

= d
{parent=P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Multiplying the system by $Q^T$ turns it into $Rx=Q^Tb$. For nonsingular $A$, compute the transformed right side and solve the upper triangular system by <back substitution>. <Orthogonal> transformations preserve Euclidean lengths and do not amplify errors in that transformation.

If $A$ is singular,
$$
0=\det A=\det Q\det R=\det Q\prod_{i=1}^nr_{ii}.
$$
Since $\det Q=\pm1$, at least one diagonal entry of $R$ is zero. The resulting triangular system must then be checked for consistency and any free variables, rather than applying divisions by zero.

= R
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= R2
{parent=R}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=R2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Suppose a <graph> satisfying <Ore's theorem> were not Hamiltonian. Extend it, on the same vertices, to an <edge>-maximal non-<Hamiltonian graph> $H$. Adding edges preserves the degree-sum hypothesis. The <complete graph> is Hamiltonian, so $H$ has a missing <edge> $uv$. By maximality, $H+uv$ has a <Hamiltonian cycle>, which must use the new <edge>. Removing it gives a <Hamiltonian path> in $H$, labelled $u=v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n=v$.

If some $i$ has both edges $uv_{i+1}$ and $vv_i$, the path can be closed into the cycle
$$
v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_i,v_n,v_{n-1},\ldots,v_{i+1},v_1,
$$
contradicting the choice of $H$. Thus the index sets $\{i:uv_{i+1}\in E(H)\}$ and $\{i:vv_i\in E(H)\}$ are disjoint subsets of $\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$. Their sizes are $\deg_Hu$ and $\deg_Hv$. Consequently $\deg_Hu+\deg_Hv\leq n-1$, contradicting the degree-sum condition for the nonadjacent pair $u,v$. Hence the original <graph> is Hamiltonian.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take a <complete graph> on vertices $a,b,c,d$ and add a fifth <vertex> $v$ joined only to $a$ and $b$. The minimum degree is two, which is less than $5/2$. The only nonadjacent pairs are $(v,c)$ and $(v,d)$, and each has degree sum $2+3=5$. Thus Ore's condition holds even though the minimum-degree condition fails. For example,
$$
\boxed{v,a,c,d,b,v}
$$
is a <Hamiltonian cycle> in this <graph>.

= b
{parent=R2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Represent the two sides of the marriage problem as the parts $L,R$ of a <bipartite graph>, with an <edge> for each allowed pairing. A <matching> is a set of edges with no common endpoint; a complete <matching> of $L$ covers every <vertex> in $L$. When the two parts have equal size it also covers $R$, giving a <perfect matching>.

<Hall's marriage theorem> gives the exact finite-<graph> criterion:
$$
\boxed{|N(S)|\geq|S|\quad\text{for every }S\subseteq L.}
$$
Here $N(S)$ is the set of right-side neighbours of $S$. Necessity follows because distinct matched vertices of $S$ need distinct neighbours; Hall's theorem supplies sufficiency.

= c
{parent=R2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $k=\chi(G)$. For $k\leq1$ the bound is immediate. Otherwise choose a <vertex>-minimal subgraph $H$ with <chromatic number> $k$. Each <vertex> has degree at least $k-1$ in $H$: if one had degree at most $k-2$, a $(k-1)$-colouring of the smaller <graph> obtained by deleting it could be extended to it using an absent neighbour colour, contradicting $\chi(H)=k$.

The <graph> $H$ has at least $k$ vertices. By the degree-sum identity,
$$
2m\geq2|E(H)|=\sum_{v\in H}\deg_Hv\geq(k-1)|V(H)|\geq k(k-1).
$$
Solving the quadratic inequality gives
$$
\boxed{\chi(G)=k\leq\frac12+\sqrt{2m+\frac14}.}
$$

= d
{parent=R2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Root the <tree> at any <vertex> and order vertices so every parent precedes its children. The root has $k$ colour choices. Each of the other $n-1$ vertices has exactly one earlier adjacent <vertex>, its parent, and therefore exactly $k-1$ choices. There are no other restrictions because a <tree> has no cycles and no extra edges between those branches. Multiplication yields
$$
\boxed{P_G(k)=k(k-1)^{n-1}.}
$$
The formula also includes the one-<vertex> <tree>.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the <complete graph> every pair of vertices is adjacent, so all $n$ colours assigned to vertices must be distinct. Choose colours successively, leaving one fewer choice at each step. Thus
$$
\boxed{P_{K_n}(k)=k(k-1)\cdots(k-n+1).}
$$
For nonnegative integer $k<n$ the product is zero, as required.

= S
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Simulation}

= S2
{parent=S}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=S2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**The assertion is false for the algorithm as printed.** Its second output depends only on $V$. For $0<V\leq1/2$ its sine factor is nonnegative. For $1/2<V<1$, its radius is at most $\sqrt{2\log2}$, so in every case $Y\geq-\sqrt{2\log2}$. Therefore $\Pr(Y<-2)=0$, whereas a standard normal variable has positive <probability> below $-2$. The printed output cannot be a pair of independent standard normals.

The minimal correction is to use $U$ in both logarithms. Let $R=\sqrt{-2\log U}$, $\Theta=2\pi V$, and set $X=R\cos\Theta$, $Y=R\sin\Theta$. Independence of $U,V$ gives independent $R,\Theta$ with densities $re^{-r^2/2}$ for $r>0$ and $1/(2\pi)$ for the angle. The polar-coordinate Jacobian is $r$, so the joint Cartesian density is
$$
\boxed{f_{X,Y}(x,y)=\frac1{2\pi}e^{-(x^2+y^2)/2}
=\frac{e^{-x^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\frac{e^{-y^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}.}
$$
This proves independent standard normal outputs for the corrected <Box-Muller transform>. The private transcription retains the source's original $\log V$ and records this correction separately.

= b
{parent=S2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the independent standard normals stipulated in this part, simulate $n$ values and average the exceedance indicators:
$$
\boxed{\widehat z_0=\frac1n\sum_{k=1}^n\mathbf1_{\{X_k>c\}}.}
$$
Each indicator is Bernoulli with <probability> $z_c$, so <expectation> and independence give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E\widehat z_0=z_c,\qquad v_0=\operatorname{Var}(\widehat z_0)=\frac{z_c(1-z_c)}n.}
$$
Thus the Monte Carlo estimator is unbiased.

= c
{parent=S2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\phi$ denote the standard normal density. Under the shifted sampling density $q(y)=\phi(y-c)$, the <likelihood ratio> is $\phi(y)/q(y)=e^{-cy+c^2/2}$. Simulate independent $Y_k\sim N(c,1)$ and use
$$
\boxed{\widehat z_c=\frac1n\sum_{k=1}^n\mathbf1_{\{Y_k>c\}}e^{-cY_k+c^2/2}.}
$$
Multiplying the weight by $q$ restores the original density, so each summand $H$ has <expectation> $\int_c^\infty\phi(y)\,dy=z_c$. The estimator is unbiased. For the second moment,
$$
\mathbb E_qH^2=\int_c^\infty e^{-cy+c^2/2}\phi(y)\,dy
\leq e^{-c^2/2}z_c,
$$
because $y\geq c$. Since $nv_c=\mathbb E_qH^2-z_c^2$,
$$
\boxed{nv_c+z_c^2\leq e^{-c^2/2}z_c.}
$$
For $c>0$, $e^{-c^2/2}<1$ and $z_c>0$, so $nv_c<z_c-z_c^2=nv_0$, proving $v_c<v_0$. Nonnegativity of <variance> also implies $z_c^2\leq e^{-c^2/2}z_c$; division by the positive $z_c$ gives
$$
\boxed{z_c\leq e^{-c^2/2}.}
$$

= T
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Linear Programming}

= T2
{parent=T}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=T2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With nonnegative production amounts $x_1,x_2,x_3$, the primal linear programme is
$$
\boxed{\max 5x_1+6x_2+3x_3,\qquad4x_1+2x_2\leq6,\quad2x_1+3x_2+2x_3\leq11.}
$$
Introduce slacks $s_R,s_S\geq0$. The initial basic variables are the slacks, and the reduced profits of the goods are $5,6,3$. The greatest is six, so $x_2$ enters. The <ratio test> compares $6/2=3$ and $11/3$, so $s_R$ leaves. The resulting dictionary is
$$
x_2+2x_1+\tfrac12s_R=3,\qquad
s_S-4x_1+2x_3-\tfrac32s_R=2,
$$
with profit $18-7x_1+3x_3-3s_R$. The greatest positive reduced profit is now three, so $x_3$ enters; its only positive pivot coefficient is two in the second row, and $s_S$ leaves. The final tableau, expressed as equations, is
$$
\begin{aligned}
x_2+2x_1+\tfrac12s_R&=3,\\
x_3-2x_1-\tfrac34s_R+\tfrac12s_S&=1,\\
P&=21-x_1-\tfrac34s_R-\tfrac32s_S.
\end{aligned}
$$
All nonbasic reduced profits are nonpositive, proving optimality at
$$
\boxed{(x_1,x_2,x_3)=(0,3,1),\qquad P=21.}
$$
The ordered optimal <basis> is $(x_2,x_3)$, hence
$$
\boxed{B=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\3&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1/2&0\\-3/4&1/2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The inverse is read from the two slack columns of the final basic equations.

= b
{parent=T2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The dual prices $u,v\geq0$ solve
$$
\boxed{\min 6u+11v,\qquad4u+2v\geq5,\quad2u+3v\geq6,\quad2v\geq3.}
$$
The optimal tableau gives $(u,v)=c_B^TB^{-1}=(6,3)B^{-1}=(3/4,3/2)$. This <vector> is dual feasible and has objective $21$, equal to the primal value, proving optimality.

Thus an extra unit of R is worth $3/4$ while the current <basis> stays feasible. Increasing its supply by $\delta$ changes the basic values to
$$
B^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}6+\delta\\11\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}3+\delta/2\\1-3\delta/4\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For increases, feasibility is retained exactly up to $\delta=4/3$. Therefore the firm can buy **up to $4/3$ extra units at a marginal value of $3/4$ per unit**. The reduced profits do not change during this right-hand-side variation.

For the proposed fourth good, the reduced profit is $8-(4u+4v)=8-9=-1$. Its profit is below the resource opportunity cost, so **it is not worth producing at the current optimum**. The old dual solution also satisfies the new good's inequality, certifying that introducing it cannot improve the optimum.

= c
{parent=T2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the $(u,v)$ plane the feasible set lies in the first quadrant above the three lines $v=3/2$, $v=5/2-2u$ and $v=2-(2/3)u$. Its lower boundary has vertices
$$
A=(3/4,3/2),\qquad B=(3/8,7/4),\qquad C=(0,5/2),
$$
and the region is unbounded upward and to the right. The objective at these vertices is respectively $21$, $43/2$ and $55/2$, so $A$ is optimal for the original supply.

\Image[2011-as2-dual-feasible.png]
{title=Dual feasible region and its three finite boundary vertices}

Let the available amount of R be $r\geq0$. Comparing the three dual <vertex> objectives gives the optimal value
$$
\boxed{V(r)=\begin{cases}
3r/4+33/2,&0\leq r\leq22/3,\\
3r/8+77/4,&22/3\leq r\leq22,\\
55/2,&r\geq22.
\end{cases}}
$$
Thus the marginal shadow price of R is $3/4$ below $22/3$, then $3/8$ between $22/3$ and $22$, and zero beyond $22$. At the two breakpoints, optimal dual prices range between the adjacent one-sided prices: $[3/8,3/4]$ and $[0,3/8]$ respectively. At zero supply the right marginal value is $3/4$; dual multipliers on the zero resource can be nonunique. These slope changes describe why the first extra $4/3$ units from the original supply six have the original price, but subsequent units do not.

= U
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistical Programming}

= U2
{parent=U}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=U2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Compare every complete row with the given <vector>, returning as soon as a match is found. The length check prevents recycling of the <vector>; `seq_len` also handles an empty <matrix> correctly. This implementation treats a missing comparison as a nonmatch and always returns a Boolean.

```
row.match <- function(X, y) {
  stopifnot(is.matrix(X), length(y) == ncol(X))
  for (i in seq_len(nrow(X))) {
    eq <- X[i, ] == y
    if (all(!is.na(eq) & eq)) return(TRUE)
  }
  FALSE
}
```

For the integer-coordinate matrices used below there are no missing values, so this is ordinary exact row equality.

= b
{parent=U2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The recursive worker extends a current path by one uniformly selected unused neighbour. A trapped extension returns `NULL` all the way to the outer loop, which restarts from the origin. It does not backtrack and try a different branch of the same attempt, since the stated process restarts the entire walk.

```
saw <- function(N) {
  stopifnot(length(N) == 1L, is.finite(N), N >= 0,
            N == floor(N))
  steps <- rbind(c(1L, 0L), c(-1L, 0L),
                 c(0L, 1L), c(0L, -1L))
  grow <- function(path) {
    if (nrow(path) == N + 1L) return(path)
    last <- path[nrow(path), ]
    candidates <- sweep(steps, 2L, last, "+")
    keep <- vapply(seq_len(nrow(candidates)), function(i) {
      !row.match(path, candidates[i, ])
    }, logical(1))
    candidates <- candidates[keep, , drop = FALSE]
    if (nrow(candidates) == 0L) return(NULL)
    j <- sample.int(nrow(candidates), 1L)
    grow(rbind(path, candidates[j, ]))
  }
  repeat {
    path <- grow(matrix(c(0L, 0L), nrow = 1L))
    if (!is.null(path)) return(path)
  }
}
```

The base case has exactly $N+1$ visited vertices. Every extension is a unit horizontal or vertical step to a new <vertex>, proving the output dimensions and self-avoidance by induction. A straight path has positive <probability>, so each independent attempt has a positive chance of success and repeated attempts terminate almost surely.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The scheme is not uniform in general.** A particular successful path has <probability> equal to the product of the reciprocals of its available-neighbour counts. Those counts depend on the path geometry.

For length four, the straight path $(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(3,0),(4,0)$ has <probability> $1/(4\cdot3\cdot3\cdot3)=1/108$. The path $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(0,1),(-1,1)$ has only two available neighbours at its last choice, because the previous point and the origin block two directions. Its <probability> is $1/(4\cdot3\cdot3\cdot2)=1/72$. There is no trapping before four steps, so these are already the output probabilities. They differ, disproving uniformity on $S_4$.

At lengths where restarts occur, conditioning an attempt on success normalizes these path probabilities by a common success <probability>; it does not remove the unequal products.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A simple square-lattice polygon has even perimeter at least four. Generate a <self-avoiding walk> of length $M-1$, reject unless its endpoint is a neighbour of the origin, then add the final <edge> to close it.

```
sap <- function(M) {
  stopifnot(length(M) == 1L, is.finite(M), M >= 4,
            M == floor(M), M %% 2 == 0)
  repeat {
    path <- saw(M - 1L)
    if (sum(abs(path[nrow(path), ])) == 1L) {
      return(rbind(path, c(0L, 0L)))
    }
  }
}
```

The output has $M+1$ coordinate rows and $M$ unit edges. The first $M$ vertices are distinct by the call to `saw`; the last row repeats only the origin. Neighbouring lattice edges cannot create an additional crossing without a repeated <vertex>. A rectangle of perimeter $M$ exists for every permitted even $M$, giving a positive <probability> of eventual acceptance.
